 the acquisition of  MCA by Seagram and he and Wasserman  never did have any kind of a  relationship Wasserman became chairman  emeritus he was still went to work every  day nobody consulted him Bronfman said  about sort of just trying to change the  company completely he wanted to do  everything different than the way  Wasserman had done that he was a  newcomer in Hollywood and seemed very  arrogant actually to think that he who  was a young man a newcomer he disparaged  everything was Herman it down it was not  a good attitude and eventually he failed  and the company was sold to Vivendi and  Wasserman was just I mean for Wasserman  who would built this you know giant  really to be there because he still came  to work every day through all these  transitions and just see the things sort  of being taken apart piece by piece  was extraordinarily painful he was  famous part of his legend that he had  created his persona was that he was  somebody who never made a mistake and I  was very surprised when I was talking to  him one day and he was recounting the  things he loved to recount his  acquisition of the Paramount film  library how great that was his  acquisition of Universal and then there  was a pause and he said now you want to  know the dumb thing I did which really  took my breath away because whilst  Herman was never supposed to say he  didn't done anything domine said you  know sold the company to the Japanese  and  who owns it today well today it's  Vivendi who well let's see Messier was  fired so I don't even remember who the  chairman of of India is today Barry  Diller has been at the helm at Universal  and they're trying to vendhya is trying  to sell it off and it'll be in pieces it  seems like pretty sure Edgar Bronfman  actually has reappeared trying to maybe  make a bid for some parts of it how long  did it take you to do this book five  years and what was the hardest part of  it the hardest part really was the  historic part because I had in my  previous books I was dealing with things  that were pretty much contemporary and  here trying to find out what had  happened in Chicago in the 20s and 30s  where there were very very few people  left who were alive then in positions  that they would have known anything that  was the hardest part but I was fortunate  to find some people who were alive and  also to find some you know oral  histories and archival material about  Capone's Chicago so that actually that  became one of my favorite parts of the  book where were you born in New Jersey  in a small town Carney you grew up in  that town or did you go to New York  immensely I grew up in that town well  and then West Orange my parents moved to  West Orange what'd your parents do my  father was a real estate person and my  mother a schoolteacher and where did you  go to school I went to Wellesley my  first two years and then I left and went  to Barnard finished up there and  Columbia Journalism school and how did  you get into this journalism business  what was your first job well after  Columbia Journalism I moved to  California and I started writing for the  San Diego reader which paid me ten  dollars for a story and then eventually  I graduated to things like human  behavior magazine which I remember paid  me $500 for a story on which I worked  for about six months my husband was  supporting me at that time obviously I  couldn't have worked for $10 for a story  and then I moved back to New York and  went to work  that's four year and then went to work  for zebra at the American Lawyer he had  just started the American Lawyer how  long were you there I was there for  about from about eighty to eighty eight  when I went to The New Yorker of all the  stuff you've written all the articles  which one got you the most controversy  well the Milken book was the most  predatory ocean predators ball was the  most controversial because Milken was  still very powerful at the time that I  wrote it he hadn't been indicted yet and  I said that he should be indicted he  didn't appreciate that  he and his people his lawyers tried to  stop the book from being published that  was the most controversial what's next  I'm back to The New Yorker happily  writing stories there I'm doing a  profile of Elliott Abrams at the moment  and why Elliott Abrams I think he's an  interesting person really interesting  person in an incredibly powerful  position at this moment his portfolio at  the NSA is the Middle East and it's  quite a moment to have the Middle East  portfolio is there any way to describe  your approach to journalism different  than others you have a special approach  to a special way to go about it I don't  think so I mean I do have this I do like  to try to tell a story a larger story  through an individual if I can and like  so many of my colleagues I try to write  narratives you know I'm a frustrated  fiction writer if I could write fiction  I'd write fiction since I can't write  fiction I look for characters whom a  novelist would covet yeah you touched on  it earlier but after this book what's  the big picture conclusion that you  reach at the end of this book well  Hollywood I mean there could never be  four years in Hollywood people would say  who's going to be the next Lew Wasserman  because he there was no one who aspired  to power in Hollywood who imagine being  anyone other than Lew Wasserman and by  the time whilst Herman died or even a  few years before it it was cleared  there would never be at least in the  foreseeable future there will not be  another Lew Wasserman because Hollywood  has changed so much and the way it is  today no one could exert the kind of  power that Wasserman did here's the  cover of the book when Hollywood had a  king as our guest just told us about Lew  Wasserman Connie Brooke thank you very  much for joining us
 

